# Go Lint Conventions

Enabled linters (see `.golangci.yml`): `govet`, `errcheck`, `staticcheck`, `unused`, `gocritic`, `usestdlibvars`, `prealloc`, `perfsprint`, `tparallel`, `whitespace`, `fatcontext`. Fix issues before committing; run `mage scf` when validating locally.

## errcheck

Always check returned errors. Do not assign to `_` unless the error is genuinely ignorable (rare).

```go
// BAD
_, err := foo()
if err != nil { ... }

// GOOD
if _, err := foo(); err != nil { ... }
```

## perfsprint

### Integer and boolean formatting

Prefer `strconv` over `fmt.Sprintf` for integer and boolean formatting.

```go
// BAD
return fmt.Sprintf("%d", version)

// GOOD
return strconv.FormatInt(version, 10)
```

For zero-padded integers, pad `strconv.FormatInt` output instead of `fmt.Sprintf("%014d", n)`:

```go
s := strconv.FormatInt(version, 10)
if len(s) < 14 {
    s = strings.Repeat("0", 14-len(s)) + s
}
```

### String concatenation

For simple string suffixes/prefixes, use concatenation instead of `fmt.Sprintf("%s.pdf", name)`:

```go
// BAD
FileName: fmt.Sprintf("%s.pdf", cmpOr(invoice.InvoiceNumber, "invoice"))

// GOOD
FileName: cmpOr(invoice.InvoiceNumber, "invoice") + ".pdf"
```

`fmt.Sprintf` remains appropriate for multi-value or non-scalar format strings (e.g. `"%d. %s -> %s"`).

### error-format

Reserve `fmt.Errorf` for wrapping (`%w`) or format verbs (`%s`, `%d`, etc.). For static error strings, define sentinel errors in `internal/errs` — do not use inline `errors.New` or `fmt.Errorf` in application code.

```go
// BAD
return "", fmt.Errorf("invalid payment terms")

// BAD — inline static error
return "", errors.New("invalid payment terms")

// GOOD — sentinel in internal/errs
return "", errs.ErrInvalidPaymentTerms

// GOOD — wrapping
return fmt.Errorf("failed to create customer: %w", err)
```

Define sentinels in domain-scoped files under `internal/errs` (e.g. `payment_terms.go`, `invoice.go`). Use `errors.Is` in tests and handlers to match them.

### concat-loop

Do not concatenate strings with `+=` inside a loop. Use `strings.Join`, `strings.Builder`, or `fmt.Appendf` on a `[]byte` buffer instead.

```go
// BAD
input := ""
for i, id := range ids {
    if i > 0 {
        input += ","
    }
    input += id
}

// GOOD
input := strings.Join(ids, ",")

// GOOD (when building incrementally without a pre-sized slice)
var b strings.Builder
for i, id := range ids {
    if i > 0 {
        b.WriteByte(',')
    }
    b.WriteString(id)
}
input := b.String()
```

## usestdlibvars

Use named constants from the standard library instead of raw literals.

```go
// BAD
w.WriteHeader(200)
time.Sleep(time.Duration(5) * time.Second)

// GOOD
w.WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)
time.Sleep(5 * time.Second)
```

## prealloc

Preallocate slices when the final length is known or bounded.

```go
// BAD
var result []Brand
for _, b := range brands {
    result = append(result, mapBrand(b))
}

// GOOD
result := make([]Brand, 0, len(brands))
for _, b := range brands {
    result = append(result, mapBrand(b))
}
```

## fatcontext

Do not call `context.WithTimeout`, `context.WithCancel`, or `context.WithDeadline` inside a loop. Create the derived context once outside the loop.

```go
// BAD
for _, item := range items {
    ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 5*time.Second)
    defer cancel()
    process(ctx, item)
}

// GOOD
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 5*time.Second)
defer cancel()
for _, item := range items {
    process(ctx, item)
}
```

## tparallel

Call `t.Parallel()` at the start of table-driven subtests (and the parent test when safe) to speed up the suite.

```go
func TestFoo(t *testing.T) {
    t.Parallel()
    for _, tt := range tests {
        t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            t.Parallel()
            // ...
        })
    }
}
```

## whitespace

Do not put a blank line immediately after a function's opening `{`.

```go
// BAD
func TestFoo(t *testing.T) {

    migrationDir := t.TempDir()
}

// GOOD
func TestFoo(t *testing.T) {
    migrationDir := t.TempDir()
}
```

## modernize (min / max)

Go 1.21+ built-in `min` and `max` replace manual compare-and-assign for bounds. Prefer them when clamping a single value.

```go
// BAD
listPage := q.Page
if listPage < 1 {
    listPage = 1
}

// GOOD
listPage := max(q.Page, 1)
```

Use `models.ClampPage` (or similar) when the upper bound depends on total count and page size—not when only enforcing a minimum of 1.

## file-layout

Declare all `type`, `struct`, and `interface` definitions at the top of each file, before `const`, `var`, and functions — including `cmd/*.go` CLI commands.

## gocritic (ifElseChain)

Rewrite `if` / `else if` / `else` chains with three or more branches to a `switch` (often `switch { case ... }` when conditions are unrelated).

```go
// BAD
if state.PendingMigration {
    ...
} else if len(state.PendingScripts) == 0 {
    ...
} else {
    ...
}

// GOOD
switch {
case state.PendingMigration:
    ...
case len(state.PendingScripts) == 0:
    ...
default:
    ...
}
```

## staticcheck (SA1019) — valgo

Use current valgo string validators; deprecated methods are flagged by staticcheck.

```go
// BAD — deprecated, removed in valgo v1.0
v.String(pw, "password").OfLengthBetween(8, 32)
v.String(data.MobileNo, "mobile number").OfLength(13)

// GOOD
v.String(pw, "password").LengthBetween(8, 32)
v.String(data.MobileNo, "mobile number").Length(13)
```
